<h2>Creating a Skill</h2>
<p>A <strong>Skill</strong> is a unit of functionality that stores a task you will repeat at least once so you can keep reusing it. Turn the design system you built earlier into a Skill and you can easily stamp out many sites with the same design later on.</p>

<h3>@ (the at sign) — referencing files and folders</h3>
<p>To have it work from an existing file, type <code>@</code>. Typing <code>@</code> brings up a file picker; choose the file you want and confirm it with <strong>Tab</strong>.</p>
<pre><code><span class="c"># Turn the design system file into a Skill</span>
@design-system_new.html Turn this design system into a "my-design" skill.</code></pre>
<p>A file or folder after <code>@</code> means "work from this content." You can point to folders as well as files.</p>

<h3>Permission requests</h3>
<p>When it is about to do something to a particular folder mid-task, <strong>options asking for permission</strong> appear.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Option 1</strong> — allow just this once.</li>
    <li><strong>Option 2</strong> — stop asking for the same kind of task.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Where Skills are stored</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Project-only</strong> — created under the project in <code>.claude/skills/</code> (available only in that project).</li>
    <li><strong>Global</strong> — under your user folder at <code>~/.claude/skills/</code> (on Windows, <code>Users\&lt;user&gt;\.claude\skills</code>). Usable no matter which folder you run from.</li>
</ul>
<p>Once the Skill is created, its content (for example, the Apple-toned white design system) is recorded under <code>~/.claude/skills/my-design/</code>.</p>

<h3>Building another site with the Skill</h3>
<pre><code>@papers_new.md Based on this paper content, use the my-design skill
to build a paper showcase website with the same design.</code></pre>
<p>This gives you a paper showcase site with the <strong>same design</strong> as the Mythos site you built earlier.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">More Skills</span>
    Creating Skills works much the same way every time. For example, give it the developer documentation URL for OpenAI's <strong>GPT Image 2</strong> and say "turn this into a GPT image skill," and you get an infographic generation skill. Formats that are not officially supported, such as <strong>HWP</strong>, can also be turned into a Skill yourself and reused.
</div>
